skewness — Sample skewness                                             data · tier 1 · since 0.5.0 · 1.0 · closed form

USAGE
    data.skewness(var, adjusted: Bool = true)

DESCRIPTION
    The third standardised moment of the values present: with m₂ and m₃ the second and third moments about
    the mean, the plain estimate is g1 = m₃ / m₂^(3/2), and the adjusted estimate G1 = g1 √(n(n − 1)) / (n −
    2) corrects it for the sample size. The adjusted form is what SPSS and Excel's SKEW report and is the
    default; `adjusted: false` gives g1, what Stata's `summarize, detail` and R's `moments::skewness`
    report. Positive for a long right tail, zero for a symmetric sample. The moments are taken over the
    values present, with the reducers' warning for the rows with `NA` skipped (section 4.1 of the Language
    Reference). The adjusted form needs three values and the plain one two: fewer give `NA` with a warning,
    and so does a sample with no spread, where the ratio is 0 / 0, so that a grouped summary over small
    groups still prints.

ARGUMENTS
    data        Frame. The frame.
    var         Var. The variable.
    adjusted:   Bool = true. The sample-size correction G1 (SPSS, Excel); `false` for g1 (Stata, moments).

VALUE
    The skewness, a Float, printed as `mean` prints.

DETAILS
    Joanes and Gill's g1 and G1 (their b1 is not offered); the moments are the means of the squared and
    cubed deviations from the mean, each a compensated sum, and n is the count of values present.

WARNINGS AND NOTES
    W0205   an operation with no value gave NA
    Z0401   the variable is not Bool, Int or Float
    Z0203   `adjusted:` is `NA`
    Z0106   the frame has a variable called `var`, the name of a parameter of this function; rename the
            variable
    W0201   missing values skipped, with the count; all missing, or no rows at all, gives NA
    W0204   Inf and -Inf both present, so the result is NA
    Z0101   no such variable in the frame
    Z0102   a variable that differs only in case from the name written

ACCURACY
    Closed form.

REFERENCES
    Joanes, D. N. and Gill, C. A. (1998) Comparing measures of sample skewness and kurtosis, Journal of the Royal Statistical Society, Series D (The Statistician) 47(1), 183–189.
    Stata, `summarize` with the `detail` option; IBM SPSS Statistics, `DESCRIPTIVES` and `FREQUENCIES`; Microsoft Excel, SKEW.

SEE ALSO
    kurtosis, mean, sd, median

EXAMPLES
    > let d = frame(x = [2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9])
    > d.skewness(x)
    0.818488
    > d.skewness(x, adjusted: false)
    0.65625
